Well, that is certainly a sensible interpretation of the question, but
given the question's vagueness I think my answer is also valid.  The OP
just asks, "What is the chance of 'that' happening?"  "That" could be
getting the second wish after having gotten the first one (your answer),
getting both wishes in two consecutive #sits, considered just before
those two #sits (my answer), or even something like "getting both wishes
in two consecutive #sits after any number of non-wish #sits" (a third
answer which no one has given; this interpretation also takes possible
pre-wish #sits into account in a fairly reasonable way).  Each
interpretation answers a valid question and gives some useful
information.  It all depends on what you consider part of the priors and
what you consider to be the event.

Similarly, the poster could have asked, "I was rolling a die, and I
rolled a 6, and then another 6.  What are the chances of that?"  The
answer could be 1/6, 1/36, or something else.  It doesn't seem to me
that you can say that 1/6 is the one correct, non-misleading answer
given that ambiguous phrasing, without clarifying the question or
reading the questioner's mind.

Also, you made a mistake in your other post.  The chance of the throne
surviving is 2/3, not 1/3, so your answer should be 2/117, not 1/117.


